content.text:
I reviewed this from the durable-objects skill guidance. The Cloudflare tools in this session only dry-run account API calls, so none of them could check DO code, and the review below is analysis only.

## Problems

1. **`setTimeout` doesn't survive eviction.** An idle Durable Object gets evicted long before an hour has passed, and the in-memory timer is lost with it. Every request also starts its own timer, so if the object did stay alive, N requests would trigger N flushes.
2. **`alarm()` never runs.** Nothing calls `setAlarm()`, so the handler is dead code. Schedule the flush with `ctx.storage.setAlarm()`.
3. **Calling `setAlarm()` on every request would starve the flush.** `setAlarm()` replaces the existing alarm. Under steady traffic, the deadline would keep moving an hour out and never fire. Check `getAlarm()` first and only set an alarm when none exists.
4. **A failed POST counts as success.** `fetch` doesn't throw on a 4xx or 5xx response. Alarms only retry when the handler throws, so a sink error drops the batch. Throw on `!res.ok`.
5. **`pending` is never cleared.** Every flush resends the whole list. Clearing it naively has its own bug: items added while `await fetch` is in progress would be deleted along with the sent ones. Remove only the items that were sent.
6. **An empty batch is still sent.** If `pending` is undefined, the code POSTs `"null"`. Return early when there is nothing to send.
7. **Delivery is at-least-once.** An alarm can be retried after the POST reached the sink, so the sink should dedupe on a batch ID.

The `count` read-then-write is fine because input gates serialize it.

## Suggested version

```ts
import { DurableObject } from "cloudflare:workers";

const FLUSH_MS = 60 * 60 * 1000;

export class Room extends DurableObject {
  async fetch(request: Request) {
    const count = ((await this.ctx.storage.get<number>("count")) ?? 0) + 1;
    await this.ctx.storage.put("count", count);
    if ((await this.ctx.storage.getAlarm()) === null) {
      await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + FLUSH_MS);
    }
    return new Response(String(count));
  }

  async alarm() {
    await this.flush();
  }

  async flush() {
    const pending = (await this.ctx.storage.get<string[]>("pending")) ?? [];
    if (pending.length === 0) return;

    const batchId = crypto.randomUUID();
    const res = await fetch("https://example.com/sink", {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "content-type": "application/json", "idempotency-key": batchId },
      body: JSON.stringify(pending),
    });
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`sink returned ${res.status}`); // alarm retries

    // Drop only what was sent; keep items appended during the fetch.
    const current = (await this.ctx.storage.get<string[]>("pending")) ?? [];
    const rest = current.slice(pending.length);
    await this.ctx.storage.put("pending", rest);
    if (rest.length > 0) await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + FLUSH_MS);
  }
}
```

The `slice` only works if every write to `pending` appends to the end. If anything else edits the list, store each item under its own key or in a SQLite row and delete by ID. For new code, the skill also recommends SQLite storage (`new_sqlite_classes`) and RPC methods over `fetch()`.
